## The sequencing decision, so you can overrule it if you disagree

**I am holding the entire migration wave — 590's apply (D-4) and 588/589's registration
(D-5/D-10) — until M0-T11 lands the migrate.ts entrypoint guard.** Neither ruling asked me to.

The reason is your own D-9 part 1: you rated T11 HIGH *precisely because* D-4 and D-5 put a
KĀRAKA to work in the hazard zone — migrations tree, `DATABASE_URL` live. Dispatching that
KĀRAKA before the guard lands would be walking into the hazard the ruling named, while the
repair for it sits half-finished in the next slot. D-9 part 2 says the field is currently
cleared, which is true and is exactly why this costs nothing: there is no urgency that would
justify running the migrator on the unfixed runner.

If you want the wave to go now, say so and I will dispatch immediately.

Both tasks are written and queued with your conditions carried verbatim — 590 with `--target`
pinned and D-4 condition 3 as a **pre-flight** simulation (the four shared-domain assets in
higher layers must be shown to land R3/R3/R5/R5 *before* the apply, since your condition says
a wrong backfill must not be applied — a post-check cannot satisfy that); 588/589 by running
the tracked runner per D-10 part 4, with `build_protected_assets` count re-verified at zero
immediately before, since your measurement will be an hour old by then.

## Three escalations

### 1. M0-T3 says two of D-6's conditions are not clean passes. It did not resolve them, correctly.

- **Condition 1** requires an orphaned row be closed to a state that "says the run died". But
  all three orphans have a `build_run_assets` row reading `state='complete'` with `ended_at`
  bit-identical to `last_built_at`. **The run did not die — the promotion did.** Closing them
  to a died-state would be as false as closing them to `lit`. M0-T3 declined to propose `lit`
  (a completed-run record is a proxy, not the promotion predicate's verdict — H4) and
  recommends re-dispatch so the state is *earned*, with `stale` as the fallback. That reasoning
  looks right to me but the ruling is yours.
- **Condition 5** scopes the repair to `asset_throughput`, yet the medians live in
  `build_run_assets` and `estimated_seconds` is an `asset_registry` column. The condition as
  written cannot cover the work it authorizes.

### 2. T-2 is parked and the collision is real: M0's exit criteria vs charter P1.

M0 requires "throughput rows on inactive assets = zero". The only such rows — all three — belong
to `ka_gochara_sweep`, whose corpus is P1's named unrecoverable asset. An exit criterion cannot
be met without touching a row on the one asset the charter reserves. Not mine.

### 3. An undetected green, found in passing by M0-T2.

Three `service` assets read `service_health='healthy'` with **no `health_probe`**. That is a
status with no detector behind it — H4/§N.8, the campaign's founding defect, sitting in the
registry right now. It belongs to R0 by layer (all three are substrate, if I read the census
right) and R0 is not open, so **I am not touching it** and have not queued it. But it should be
recorded somewhere durable now rather than rediscovered at R0's intake, and where that record
lives is your call.

## Two governance notes, no action needed from me

- Three separate commit races have now swept in-flight files into siblings' commits
  (`079194b74`, `6d4e548af`, `69ba600c7`). Content verified byte-identical every time and no one
  rewrote history to tidy it, which is the right instinct (H2). Your D-3 path-scoping rule is
  being followed; the remaining hole is that path-scoped `git add` still commits whatever a
  sibling has *already staged*. I have started telling every KĀRAKA to check `git diff --cached`
  immediately before committing. If you want that as precedent rather than my house style,
  it is yours to rule.
- M0-T2 flagged that the mailbox is gitignored, so KĀRAKA reports and findings are not
  version-controlled — and declined to `git add -f` its own report on the grounds that
  overriding the repo's ignore policy to make its own work look committed is not its call.
  Correct instinct. Whether the durable trace those reports carry should live somewhere tracked
  is a governance question I am flagging, not deciding.

